WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.667 --> 00:00:04.537
- We look at every subject
that we want to film

00:00:04.537 --> 00:00:06.951
and then we say, "Well,
what is the best way

00:00:06.951 --> 00:00:08.368
"to get into their world?"

00:00:08.368 --> 00:00:10.702
It's an amazing moment when you realize

00:00:10.702 --> 00:00:14.869
that you are actually seeing
some stuff for the first time.

00:00:18.786 --> 00:00:22.213
My name's John Downer, and these are my

00:00:22.213 --> 00:00:24.963
incredible team of spy creatures.

00:00:26.277 --> 00:00:27.778
Well, I suppose it all
started when I started

00:00:27.778 --> 00:00:30.281
making wildlife films,
and I wanted to have that

00:00:30.281 --> 00:00:32.882
kind of intimate perspective
to get into the animal world

00:00:32.882 --> 00:00:35.325
and actually know what
it's like to be an animal.

00:00:35.325 --> 00:00:37.362
Over the years, used technology to get

00:00:37.362 --> 00:00:39.413
more and more inside the animal world.

00:00:39.413 --> 00:00:41.319
As things have progressed we've moved

00:00:41.319 --> 00:00:44.445
from just using remote
cameras disguised as rocks

00:00:44.445 --> 00:00:46.571
to where we are today, which is having,

00:00:46.571 --> 00:00:48.949
you know, these incredible spy creatures,

00:00:48.949 --> 00:00:51.575
which are able to do filming,
cameras in their eyes,

00:00:51.575 --> 00:00:53.224
but they look like the animals and

00:00:53.224 --> 00:00:55.839
the animals will interact with them.

00:00:55.839 --> 00:00:58.913
The whole idea of, you know, using
the robots, uh, came about

00:00:58.913 --> 00:01:00.892
when we made a film about penguins.

00:01:00.892 --> 00:01:02.186
For the first time, we thought:

00:01:02.186 --> 00:01:04.214
"Well, what if we made a penguin cam,

00:01:04.214 --> 00:01:08.335
"a living, moving, camera
that looked like a penguin,

00:01:08.335 --> 00:01:10.021
"that was able to go into the colony

00:01:10.021 --> 00:01:12.123
"and start to get these incredible shots?”

00:01:12.123 --> 00:01:16.290
You can't explain it because
it's a dream come true.

00:01:18.424 --> 00:01:20.742
I think part of the whole technique is

00:01:20.742 --> 00:01:23.525
to try and get inside the animals' minds.

00:01:23.525 --> 00:01:27.692
Understanding animal behavior
is key to it all really.

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:33.820
We lost one spy pup to a wolf.

00:01:36.165 --> 00:01:40.578
A spy tortoise was
squashed by an elephant.

00:01:40.578 --> 00:01:41.933
And I think it's something about the fact

00:01:41.933 --> 00:01:44.314
that they look living; it makes them think

00:01:44.314 --> 00:01:45.740
before they actually do anything,

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:48.975
and if it's not a threat,
then they kind of...

00:01:48.975 --> 00:01:50.558
They quite like it.

00:01:56.844 --> 00:01:59.874
The challenges are… They’re innumerable,

00:01:59.874 --> 00:02:01.250
because everything is a challenge

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:04.643
from the beginning to the
point you get the film.

00:02:04.643 --> 00:02:06.267
The animals, once you get there,

00:02:06.267 --> 00:02:08.549
and once you're actually with them,

00:02:08.549 --> 00:02:11.812
they tend to be the least
of all the problems.

00:02:11.812 --> 00:02:15.084
They usually do deliver
because they do amazing things

00:02:15.084 --> 00:02:17.341
and if you've got the
technology to be able to film

00:02:17.341 --> 00:02:20.163
close to them in their world,

00:02:20.163 --> 00:02:24.330
you know you're going to
get something remarkable.

